  "official_timing_warning_text": "Corrected timestamps for QLP Sectors 99-104 A barycentric timing correction error was identified starting in Orbit 210 (second half of Sector 99) onward to Orbit 220 (end of Sector 104) This was in part due to changing interpolation library backends, where expected error-raising conditions no longer triggered, silencing an ephemeris file that had reached the end of its sampling range. Starting Orbit 215 (first half of Sector 102), a new ephemeris file was used; however, the expected columns were not in the same order, and barycentric timing correction was performed on spacecraft velocity values instead of position. This has been corrected using a programmatic approach that does not involve an operator downloading from JPL once a year. MAST is in the process of updating these sector files with corrected timestamps. Affected Sectors with asterisks (*) in the Sector Availability table below have not yet been redelivered. Corrected timestamps for QLP Sectors 74-79 QLP uses the TESS ephemeris as predicted by JPL horizons to correct spacecraft time to BJD TDB. A new ephemeris data file is downloaded at the beginning of each year to use for the position of the spacecraft for the duration of that year. The file downloaded for 2024 was generated using the wrong reference plane: the QLP software is designed to use coordinates based on the ICRF equatorial reference plane (i.e., the x-y plane of the ICRF), but the file was generated with coordinates based on the ecliptic plane derived from the ICRF. As a result, the barycentric correction applied to light curve timestamps was based on the wrong spacecraft position, leading to a timestamp error of up to roughly \u00b13.0 minutes. The error depends on each star\u2019s CCD position and the difference between the actual spacecraft coordinates and the coordinates from the invalid file, which varied over the course of the year. The error at the 4 CCD centers for each camera is shown in the plot below as a function of time. QLP light curves delivered to MAST for sectors 74-79 (inclusive) were affected by an ephemeris bug (denoted by asterisk). By sector 80, the invalid ephemeris file was identified and fixed, and verification methods were put in place to ensure ephemeris data used by QLP going forward is correct. Light curves for the affected sectors have been corrected and re-uploaded to MAST as version 2 (v02) data products. ",
